  "passage": "Few drinks have travelled as far as tea, and fewer still have altered the fortunes of so many nations along the way. According to one account, the leaves were first brewed by accident (1)____ than by design, when a few of them drifted into a pot of water being boiled beneath a tree. Whether or not the story is true, tea (2)____ certainly been cultivated in the hills of south-west China for well over two thousand years. For most of that time it remained a local pleasure; not (3)____ the ninth century did it become a commodity worth carrying across mountains on the backs of mules. Compressed into bricks, it was traded for horses along a route so arduous that some porters carried loads heavier (4)____ themselves.<br><br>When European merchants eventually (5)____ across the drink in the early seventeenth century, they had no idea what to make of it. Within a hundred years, however, the British were importing it (6)____ such quantities that the government came to depend on the tax it yielded. This dependence had consequences (7)____ which nobody had planned. Smuggling flourished, colonies protested, and one famous cargo ended up in a harbour rather than in a teapot. Had the tax been lighter, the course of at least one revolution (8)____ have run differently.<br><br>Today tea is grown on every inhabited continent and drunk more widely than any beverage (9)____ water. Global commodity (10)____ it now is, it has kept a curious intimacy: whatever the country, offering someone a cup still signals that they are welcome to stay.",
