  "passage": "Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that as (1)____ as half may have vanished by the end of the century. A language does not die suddenly. It fades, generation by generation, and nearly always in the same way. First, speakers of a small language come into contact with a larger one that offers access to jobs, schooling or trade. Parents, keen for their children to get (2)____ in the wider world, begin to speak the dominant language at home. The children grow up understanding their grandparents but answering them in the new tongue. By the third generation, the old language survives only in songs, prayers and a few expressions (3)____ nobody can quite translate.<br><br>It is tempting to see this as a natural process, no more to be regretted (4)____ the extinction of a dialect of Latin. Linguists disagree, and (5)____ good reason. Every language divides up experience in its own way: some have a dozen words for kinds of snow, others mark whether a speaker witnessed an event or merely heard about it. (6)____ time such a language goes, an irreplaceable record of how human beings have made sense of their surroundings goes with it.<br><br>Efforts to reverse the decline have had mixed results. Schools that teach in a minority language can slow its loss, but they cannot on their (7)____ restore it, because a language lives in kitchens and markets, not in classrooms. Success stories exist — one Pacific island language has been brought (8)____ from a handful of elderly speakers to several thousand — but they depend on something no government can legislate: a community deciding that its language is (9)____ keeping. Without that decision, even careful documentation amounts to (10)____ more than a record of a voice already fallen silent.",
