  "passage": "Few sights in nature rival thousands of geese lifting off a marsh at dawn, yet the mechanics of how they find their way are (1)____ to be fully understood. What is certain is that no single sense accounts for the feat. Birds appear to draw (2)____ a whole toolkit: the setting sun, the rotating stars, the smell of the coast and, most remarkably, the Earth's magnetic field, which they seem to perceive through specialised proteins in the eye. (3)____ sooner had researchers demonstrated this magnetic sense in the laboratory than a new puzzle arose, for a compass tells a traveller which way is north but not where it currently is. Somehow the birds manage (4)____ only to hold a heading but also to correct for being blown hundreds of kilometres off course. Young birds on their first journey seem to inherit a rough programme, an instruction to fly in a given direction for a given number of weeks, (5)____ older birds refine this with a memory of landmarks built up over previous seasons. This would explain why juveniles displaced by storms tend to end (6)____ in the wrong place while adults usually recover. The costs are severe: a small warbler may lose nearly half its body weight crossing the Sahara, and every year countless birds fail to complete the trip. (7)____ the risks, the strategy persists because the rewards, chiefly the brief but enormous abundance of insects in the northern summer, outweigh them. Nowadays, however, timing that evolution fine-tuned over millennia is being thrown (8)____ of step with a warming climate. Insects are emerging earlier, and birds (9)____ arrive on the old schedule find the feast already over. Whether they can adjust quickly enough is a question (10)____ which the answer, for many species, will decide their survival.",
