  "passage": "Ask a group of people to name a calming colour and most (1)____ them will say blue; ask for an alarming one and red will win by a wide margin. Such agreement is striking, and it has led many to conclude that colour acts on the mind directly, (2)____ of culture or experience. The evidence, however, is far from straightforward. It is true that red has been shown to raise the pulse slightly and that athletes wearing it win marginally more often, an effect (3)____ to the association between red and dominance in many species. Yet the same colour signals luck in one country and danger in another, and a bride dressed in white would be a cause for grief in cultures where white is the colour of mourning. What seems innate, in other words, may be (4)____ more than a habit learnt so early that we no longer notice having learnt it. Marketing departments have not waited for the argument to be settled. Fast-food chains favour warm colours on the (5)____ that they stimulate appetite and encourage customers to eat quickly and leave; banks lean towards navy and grey, shades that are supposed to suggest stability, (6)____ the balance sheet actually says. Whether these choices work is another matter: a company (7)____ logo is the wrong shade of orange will not go bankrupt on that account. The most robust finding is perhaps the least dramatic. Colour affects us, but only in context, only briefly, and only at the margins. Anyone who claims that painting a classroom green will make children behave has, (8)____ all likelihood, mistaken a fashion for a law of nature, and it is worth remembering that such claims (9)____ been made, and quietly abandoned, for well (10)____ a century.",
