  "passage": "Growing food where rain falls only a few times a year sounds futile, yet farmers have done precisely that for thousands of years, and their methods are attracting fresh attention as the climate warms. The oldest trick is simply to (1)____ the most of what little water there is. In the Negev, ancient farmers channelled the run-off from bare hillsides into small walled fields, (2)____ a single storm could deliver the equivalent of a season's rain. Modern versions, from drip irrigation to buried clay pots that release moisture slowly, (3)____ on the same principle: deliver water to the root and nowhere else. Choice of crop matters just as much. Date palms and millet can (4)____ levels of heat and salt that would kill wheat within days, and breeders are now combing wild relatives of common crops for drought-tolerance genes. Perhaps the most ambitious projects, however, are those that set (5)____ to reverse the desert's advance altogether. Along the Sahara's edge, rows of trees are being planted to hold back the dunes and shade the soil, keeping evaporation (6)____ a minimum and slowly restoring the organic matter that overgrazing stripped away. Critics note that such schemes have a poor record; too many have been (7)____ with great fanfare and then quietly forgotten once the funding dried up. There is also the question of who owns the water. Pumping fossil aquifers to grow fodder for export may pay in the (8)____ term, but the water took ten thousand years to accumulate and will not be replaced. (9)____ desert farming can feed the growing populations of the world's dry regions depends less on technology than on whether governments will value water as the scarce resource it is. Without that shift, even the cleverest irrigation will do little more than put (10)____ the inevitable.",
